  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1:  Severe thunderstorms are a near-certainty for \nTuesday afternoon and evening. Some areas of flash flooding are\nalso expected.\n\nFor tonight, PWAT values will increase from the south by 0.5 to\n0.75 of an inch leading to notably more humid late\nnight/Tuesday.\n\nSevere weather is once again on the slate for Tuesday afternoon\nand evening as an amplifying upper level trough heads SE across\nthe Great Lakes region and a strong, sub-1000 mb sfc low (just\nsouth of James Bay Canada Tuesday afternoon) pushes a potent\ncold front SE across PA late Tuesday/early Wed. \n\nTuesday (Day 2) has been consistently highlighted by SPC for \nmany days as a potentially active and dangerous weather day. \nThe DY2 ENH Risk area has been expanded to cover the entire\nstate of PA except for a sliver near the NY border.\n\nThe threat for pockets pf damaging wind is near certain, and \ncould occur anywhere over the CWA Tuesday afternoon and evening.\n\nAdditionally, the threat for hail is higher than it was this \npast Saturday, and the potential for tornadoes is similar, with\na mix of supercells and line segments with mini bow echoes\nexpected to be the primary storm modes given the presence of\nlikely low level boundary from some morning convection across\nthe east, followed by a several hour period of\nsubsidence/partial clearing beneath and juicing up of the\ninstability and deep layer shear as the nose of a 100+ kt 300 mb\njet approaches from the Ohio Valley late in the\nafternoon/evening. \n\n0-1KM EHIs of 2-3.5 M2/S2 with leftover llvl outflow boundaries\nand one or more prefrontal/lee-side troughs point toward a\ndistinct threat for some long-lasting supercells and potentially\na few to several EF1-2 tornadoes across our CWA.   \n\nIt looks like a pre-frontal trough will charge in from the NW \nand cross the CWA in the afternoon hours. The real cold front \nwill arrive in NW PA later in the evening/early Tuesday night. \nIt may arrive too late (some stabilization could already happen)\nand have little left to work with by the time it gets into the \nCWA to make much of a severe threat. \n\nThere is also a threat for localized flash flooding, but only \nin places that receive multiple rounds of heavy rain. There \nshould be a fairly fast movement to the storm cells (ENE at\nabout 40 kts), limiting the threat somewhat. However, moisture \nis expected to be very high, PWATs 1.5-2\". \n\n  ---------------------------------------------------------\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Thicker wildfire smoke should stay mainly over \nwestern PA, with levels barely noticeable in the east. \n\nAn air quality alert/action day remains in effect for most of\nsouthwestern PA where levels are expected to be unhealthy for\nsensitive groups. HRRR smoke plume progs keep the thicker smoke\nover western PA with much less over the east. The light wind\nthis morning will turn from easterly to southerly through the\nday which will help to keep the near-sfc smoke from spreading \nto the east.",
